Microsoft's Paradox
Case Study #3 - Microsoft Copilot

Author: Mahmoud
Frameworks: Root Cause Analysis (5 Whys), Build/Buy/Partner
Tools: AI-assisted research and framework application


CASE QUESTION

Is Copilot actually failing in the AI market, and if so, why - and what should Microsoft do about it?


HYPOTHESIS

Copilot's user numbers are inflated by integration into Windows and Microsoft 365 rather than reflecting genuine user preference. Where users have a choice, they are not choosing Copilot.


DATA - MARKET POSITION

Metric: Web traffic share (Similarweb, March 2026)
Source: Investor Rihard Jarc April 2026 analysis, cited in fatjoe.com/blog/chatgpt-stats

ChatGPT: 56.7%
Gemini: 25.5%
Claude: 6.02%
Grok: ~6.0%
DeepSeek: 3.7%
Perplexity: 2.0%
Copilot: 1.1% (last place)

Note on methodology: Web traffic share was chosen over user counts because every platform defines users differently. ChatGPT reports WAU, Gemini reports MAU, Copilot publishes no standalone figure. Similarweb applies the same methodology to all platforms, making it the only like-for-like comparison available. It undercounts platforms with heavy API usage (notably Claude) but is the most consistent data available.

Supporting data points:
- Copilot workplace conversion rate: 35.8% (share of users with access who actively choose to use it)
- ChatGPT workplace conversion rate: 83.1% for comparison
- Copilot NPS: -19.8 as of January 2026
Source: Stackmatix, stackmatix.com/blog/copilot-market-adoption-trends

Hypothesis confirmed. Copilot holds 1.1% web traffic share despite near-universal device presence. The gap between access and usage is the core finding.


FRAMEWORK 1 - ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS (5 WHYS)

Purpose: Drill beneath the surface symptom (low usage) to find the structural cause.

Why 1: Users are not choosing Copilot despite it being available everywhere.
Observation: 1.1% web traffic share against near-universal Windows/Office presence. Access is not the barrier.

Why 2: Users do not trust Copilot.
Data: NPS of -19.8, 35.8% workplace conversion rate.
Key finding: Microsoft's own consumer terms describe Copilot as "for entertainment purposes only" and state users "should not rely on it for important advice" - while simultaneously marketing it as an essential workplace tool. Enterprise documentation focuses on compliance; consumer terms focus on liability. The product sends contradictory signals to both audiences.
Source: Windows Forum, windowsforum.com/threads/microsoft-copilot-entertainment-fine-print-ai-trust-vs-legal-guardrails

Why 3: Microsoft prioritised enterprise compliance over user experience.
Copilot was built for regulated industries - financial services, healthcare, government - where data governance matters more than conversational quality. The result is a product that feels constrained to general users.

Why 4: Copilot was designed as a productivity layer, not a standalone AI competitor.
Microsoft framed Copilot from the outset as an enhancement to Word, Excel, Outlook and Teams rather than a product competing directly with ChatGPT or Claude. Product decisions - guardrails, tone, capability scope - were made to serve the Office ecosystem, not to win in the open AI market.

Why 5 (Root Cause): Microsoft lacked the AI expertise and proprietary data to compete with frontier model labs, and chose integration over competition.
Google owns Search data at a scale no competitor can match. Anthropic and OpenAI are built from the ground up around model research. Microsoft is an enterprise software company. Its core competency is distribution and enterprise relationships, not AI research or consumer product design. Copilot is the product that identity produces: an enterprise software company adding AI, not an AI company serving enterprises.

Root cause confirmed: Strategic identity mismatch. Copilot is not failing because the model is weak. It is failing because it was never built to compete.

Note: Initial hypothesis was that the underlying model was weaker than competitors. This was disproved - Microsoft 365 Copilot runs GPT-5.3/5.4, with GPT-5.5 added in May 2026. The model is not the problem.
Source: skywork.ai/skypage/en/ai-microsoft-copilot-guide, learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio


FRAMEWORK 2 - BUILD / BUY / PARTNER

Purpose: Identify how Microsoft closes the capability gap identified in the root cause analysis.

The gap: Microsoft lacks AI research expertise and proprietary training data. The question is how to acquire both.

Option 1 - Build (develop a proprietary frontier model from scratch)
Verdict: Ruled out.
Reasoning: The root cause is a lack of research talent and training data. Building requires both. Cortana is the precedent - a years-long internal AI effort that failed and was deprecated. Microsoft has already committed over $25 billion to OpenAI; building a competing internal model would conflict with that relationship and require a multi-year timeline during which competitors would not stand still.

Option 2 - Buy (acquire a frontier model lab)
Verdict: Viable in limited form, with risk.
Reasoning: A targeted acquisition brings AI research talent and independent model capability in-house. Best candidate: Mistral AI - frontier model lab, strong European regulatory standing, training data independent of OpenAI. Risk: acquiring a competing model lab destabilises the OpenAI relationship that currently powers Copilot. Acquisition alone also does not fix the strategic identity problem - buying capability without repositioning the product replicates the same mistake at higher cost.

Option 3 - Partner (diversify AI partnerships beyond OpenAI)
Verdict: Recommended as primary action.
Reasoning: Partnering accesses frontier model capability without acquisition conflict risk or build timeline risk. Recommended partner: Mistral AI. Fully independent, no existing relationship with Google or Amazon, European regulatory standing gives Microsoft a credible differentiation story in GDPR-sensitive enterprise markets. Mistral provides a genuinely independent model stack rather than a second dependency on another US hyperscaler.

Objection considered - why not deepen the OpenAI partnership?
Microsoft is already an OpenAI investor and Copilot already runs on GPT models. The obvious question is why not consolidate rather than diversify.
Rejected because: the problem is not access to GPT models - Microsoft already has that, and it has produced 1.1% market share. The deeper issue is dependence. OpenAI is actively building products that compete directly with Copilot - ChatGPT has a desktop app, memory, deep research, and a productivity suite that mirrors Office use cases. Microsoft is currently funding and powering its own competition. Deepening the OpenAI relationship entrenches that dynamic rather than resolving it. Diversification is the correct move precisely because the existing partnership is not working.

Objection considered - why not Anthropic?
Anthropic operates a deliberate multi-partner model (Google, Amazon, others) which makes exclusivity difficult to negotiate. Google's existing Anthropic investment creates a conflict of interest for Microsoft specifically, since Google is their primary enterprise AI competitor. Mistral avoids both problems.


RECOMMENDATION

Primary: Partner with Mistral AI to diversify Copilot's model stack and reduce OpenAI dependence. Mistral's European independence gives Microsoft genuine differentiation in regulated enterprise markets.
Secondary: Selective acquisition of a smaller AI research team to bring independent model research capability in-house over time.
Ruled out: Build (Cortana precedent, timeline risk). Deepening OpenAI (entrenches dependence on a direct competitor).

The strategic question Microsoft must answer: is Copilot an AI product that integrates with Office, or an Office feature that uses AI? Currently it is the latter. To compete in both consumer and enterprise AI markets, it needs to become the former.


